# Generate SDKs and artifacts

From the project containing `trellis.toml`, run:

```sh
trellis update
trellis generate
```

The project-local output is:

```text
.trellis/
├── apis/       # lock-verified installed dependency artifacts
├── artifacts/  # owned canonical API and participant artifacts
├── rust/       # private generated Rust SDKs and facades
└── ts/         # private generated TypeScript SDKs and participants
```

The language directories depend on the project manifests: `deno.json` or
`deno.jsonc` selects TypeScript; `Cargo.toml` selects Rust. A mixed project can
generate both. The tree above shows both possibilities.

`trellis generate` is offline. Local dependencies compile from source; registry
dependencies use exact installed artifacts from `trellis.lock`. A failed compile
leaves the previous successful outputs untouched.

In CI or a fresh checkout, `trellis install` reproduces locked dependency
artifacts and generates the language outputs. Use `trellis update` when you
intend to resolve dependency changes, not merely to rebuild an existing lock.
